WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:01.935
[dramatic music]

00:00:02.001 --> 00:00:05.505
NARRATOR: So what is behind
this deadly spate of attacks?

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:11.678
According to local news
reporter Jerry Sinon,

00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:14.414
it's a question on
everyone's mind.

00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:18.051
There was a lot of rumors
in regards to the attacks.

00:00:18.118 --> 00:00:19.352
Why did it happen?

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:21.788
And in two weeks time
it happened twice.

00:00:24.891 --> 00:00:27.727
NARRATOR: One such rumor
concerns the identity

00:00:27.794 --> 00:00:30.497
of the killer shark.

00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:34.434
The ferocity of the attack and
the severity of the injuries

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:37.337
incurred seem to point
toward the ocean's

00:00:37.404 --> 00:00:40.440
most fearsome predator.

00:00:40.507 --> 00:00:42.942
People were saying that
it was a white shark.

00:00:43.009 --> 00:00:46.212
[electronic music]

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:49.883
NARRATOR: Great
whites are the largest

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:52.452
predatory fish on the planet.

00:00:52.519 --> 00:00:58.391
Growing up to 20 feet in length,
they are powerful hunters,

00:00:58.458 --> 00:01:02.429
able to propel their bodies
completely out of the water

00:01:02.495 --> 00:01:05.932
when attacking prey from below.

00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:09.803
And they are responsible
for more fatal attacks

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:12.071
on humans than any other shark.

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:20.013
So is this the species
behind the spike?

00:01:20.079 --> 00:01:23.750
Determining that type of
shark may help locals prevent

00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:25.351
attacks from happening again.

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:35.962
Shark expert Dr. Enrico
Gennari studies great whites

00:01:36.029 --> 00:01:38.631
off the coast of South Africa.

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:42.569
He believes the answer to
whether a white shark could be

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:47.273
responsible for the Seychelles
attacks may come down to where

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.643
these predators go and why.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.379
White sharks travel a lot.

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:56.483
All their life is about moving.

00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:58.551
NARRATOR: Gennari
has spent the past 13

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:02.989
years tagging these predators
to track their movements.

00:02:03.056 --> 00:02:07.060
Using a long pole, he attaches
a small non-invasive device

00:02:07.126 --> 00:02:07.827
to their fins.

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:13.399
[suspenseful music]

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:19.572
ENRICO GENNARI:
OK, shark coming.

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:23.576
3, 2, 1.

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:26.513
[dramatic music]

00:02:27.614 --> 00:02:28.548
It's on.

00:02:28.615 --> 00:02:30.750
Yes!

00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:31.718
Now we track it.

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:38.057
NARRATOR: Through such tagging
studies, experts like Gennari

00:02:38.124 --> 00:02:44.030
have discovered that white
sharks are highly migratory.

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:47.233
In fact, some great
whites will swim up

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:54.541
to 1,000 miles each month, or
more than 40 miles per day,

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:57.477
to reach a destination.

00:02:57.544 --> 00:02:58.711
ENRICO GENNARI:
Often, white sharks

00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:03.249
are attracted to a specific
place because of food.

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:06.052
NARRATOR: But in
their quest for prey,

00:03:06.119 --> 00:03:10.690
white sharks don't
just go anywhere.

00:03:10.757 --> 00:03:15.261
It really depends on the
temperature in the water.

00:03:15.328 --> 00:03:17.096
NARRATOR: Unlike
most other sharks,

00:03:17.163 --> 00:03:19.832
great whites are warm-blooded.

00:03:19.899 --> 00:03:23.937
That means they always maintain
their body temperature above

00:03:24.003 --> 00:03:26.539
that of the surrounding sea.

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:29.976
So they thrive in a
particular climate.

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:32.679
White sharks usually like
water temperature quite cold.

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:37.717
NARRATOR: This is the
key to the investigation

00:03:37.784 --> 00:03:41.521
in the Seychelles, because
great whites are typically

00:03:41.588 --> 00:03:48.061
found within temperature ranges
of 54 to 75 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:55.168
But the water off of the islands
routinely reaches into the 80s.

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:57.337
White shark definitely
don't like tropical water

00:03:57.403 --> 00:03:58.438
like in Seychelles.

00:03:58.504 --> 00:04:00.473
So that shark in the
Seychelles most likely

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.475
was not a great white shark.

00:04:05.545 --> 00:04:07.847
NARRATOR: The culprit's
identity and the reason

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:14.454
it attacked remains unknown.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.890
For locals and tourists
in the Seychelles,

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:20.526
it's a disconcerting premise.

00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:22.195
It's still a
mystery and people

00:04:22.262 --> 00:04:23.730
still wanted to know why.

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:27.033
[suspenseful music]

